import json
from pathlib import Path
q=[]
def mc(l,p,options,answer,why): q.append(dict(id=len(q),lesson=l,prompt=p,options=options.split('|'),answer=answer,why=why,type='mc'))
def num(l,p,a,why): q.append(dict(id=len(q),lesson=l,prompt=p,answer=str(a),why=why,type='input'))
mc(1,'Two-sum must return distinct indices. Which input exposes an implementation that reuses the current element before checking its complement?','[3], target 6|[3,4], target 7|[1,2], target 8|[], target 0',0,'A single 3 cannot be used twice. Check for an existing complement before inserting the current element.')
mc(1,'A baseline passes 10,000 random tests. What conclusion is justified?','It is correct for all valid inputs|No tested input exposed a failure|Its worst-case bound is established|Its average cost is now constant',1,'Testing provides evidence on the tested inputs. Universal correctness and asymptotic claims require separate reasoning.')
num(1,'How many unordered index pairs does brute-force two-sum examine on 8 elements if no pair matches?',28,'Each pair i<j is visited once: 8×7/2=28.')
mc(1,'You need original indices after sorting for two-sum. What information should travel with each value?','Its original array index|Its final sorted position|Its number of comparisons|Its current neighbor value',0,'Sorting value-index pairs preserves the indices required by the contract.')
mc(2,'Solve T(n)=T(n−1)+Θ(1), T(1)=Θ(1).','Θ(log n)|Θ(n)|Θ(n log n)|Θ(n²)',1,'There are n−1 recursive reductions with constant additional work each.')
mc(2,'Solve T(n)=2T(n/2)+(n log n)².','Θ(n log²n)|Θ(n² log n)|Θ(n² log²n)|Θ(n² log³n)',2,'The n²log²n work dominates n^(log₂2)=n polynomially and satisfies regularity.')
num(2,'Merge [2,7] and [1,5]. How many element-to-element comparisons are needed before one side becomes empty?',3,'Compare 2 vs 1, 2 vs 5, and 7 vs 5; append the remaining 7 without a comparison.')
mc(2,'Which tie rule makes a conventional Merge Sort stable?','Take the left item on equality|Take the right item on equality|Alternate sides on equality|Choose the shorter half on equality',0,'Equal elements from the left half appeared earlier originally, so they must remain earlier.')
mc(3,'Which notation denotes an asymptotic upper bound?','Θ|O|Ω|ω',1,'Big O is an eventual upper bound. Θ is tight; Ω is a lower bound; little omega is a strict lower-growth relation.')
mc(3,'Which claim about f(n)=3n²+2n is false?','f is O(n³)|f is Ω(n)|f is Θ(n²)|f is Θ(n³)',3,'A quadratic is upper-bounded by a cubic, but it is not lower-bounded by a positive constant times n³ eventually.')
mc(3,'An invariant holds initially and is maintained. What else establishes total correctness?','A decreasing termination measure and the exit implication|A benchmark showing small input finishes quickly|A second invariant with the same meaning|A randomized test covering several input sizes',0,'The invariant gives partial correctness at exit. A well-founded decreasing measure proves termination.')
num(3,'How many iterations execute in “for (int i=1; i<64; i*=2)”?',6,'The body runs at i=1,2,4,8,16,32.')
mc(4,'With geometric capacity growth, dynamic-array append is:','Worst-case Θ(1), amortized Θ(1)|Worst-case Θ(n), amortized Θ(1)|Worst-case Θ(n), amortized Θ(log n)|Worst-case Θ(log n), amortized Θ(n)',1,'An occasional append copies n elements; the total copying across a sequence is linear.')
mc(4,'Why can a LinkedList scan be slower than an ArrayList scan?','Extra comparisons are required by linked lists|Pointer chasing gives poorer cache locality|Linked nodes must be visited in sorted order|Every next-node operation searches from the head',1,'Sequential nodes may be far apart in memory. Both scans are linear, but memory behavior differs.')
mc(4,'Remove all n entries using standard binary-heap root removal. Worst-case total cost?','Θ(n)|Θ(log n)|Θ(n log n)|Θ(n²)',2,'The sum of logarithmic removals is Θ(n log n), despite bottom-up construction being linear.')
num(4,'In a zero-indexed binary heap, what is the left-child index of index 4?',9,'The left child is 2i+1=9; the right child is 10.')
mc(5,'Chaining with well-distributed hashing has expected lookup cost:','O(1+α)|O(log n+α)|O(n+α)|O(α log n)',0,'A chain has expected length proportional to the load factor α=n/m.')
mc(5,'A trie key has length L and O(1) transitions. Exact lookup costs:','O(log L)|O(L)|O(n)|O(nL)',1,'One transition per symbol plus a terminal check is sufficient.')
mc(5,'A hash table exceeds its configured maximum load factor. What is the usual next step?','Keep capacity and change key equality|Allocate a larger table and rehash entries|Keep capacity and remove duplicate hashes|Allocate a larger table and preserve bucket indices',1,'Bucket indices depend on capacity, so entries must be redistributed.')
num(5,'A chained table stores 150 keys in 60 buckets. What is its load factor?',2.5,'α=n/m=150/60=2.5; chaining permits α greater than 1.')
mc(6,'Prim starts at 1 with edges 1–2(2), 1–3(3), 2–3(1), 2–4(4), 3–4(5). Which sequence is selected?','1–2, 2–3, 3–4|1–2, 2–3, 2–4|1–2, 1–3, 2–4|2–3, 1–2, 2–4',1,'Prim selects the minimum crossing edge of the current tree, not the globally smallest unused edge.')
mc(6,'Prim on a connected simple adjacency-list graph with a binary heap runs in:','O(V²)|O(E log V)|O(E+V)|O(V log V)',1,'Heap updates for graph edges contribute O(E log V). A matrix implementation has a different bound.')
mc(6,'Kruskal encounters an edge whose endpoints have the same DSU representative. It should:','Accept it and merge by rank|Reject it to avoid a cycle|Accept it if the weight is negative|Reject all remaining heavier edges',1,'The endpoints already have a path in the current forest. Adding the edge creates a cycle regardless of sign.')
num(6,'A connected undirected graph has 9 vertices. How many edges does any spanning tree contain?',8,'Every tree on V vertices has V−1 edges.')
mc(7,'Contract every SCC to one vertex. The condensation graph is always:','A tree|A complete graph|A DAG|A strongly connected graph',2,'A directed cycle between distinct SCCs would make them mutually reachable, contradicting maximality.')
mc(7,'Which procedure directly produces a topological order of a DAG?','Prim’s algorithm|Kahn’s algorithm|Bellman–Ford|Disjoint set union',1,'Kahn removes zero-indegree vertices. It also detects cycles if not all vertices can be removed.')
mc(7,'A vertex finish time in DFS is recorded when:','It is first discovered|Its first outgoing edge is explored|All its DFS descendants are processed|All its DFS ancestors are processed',2,'Finish occurs after every outgoing exploration from that vertex has completed.')
num(7,'Kahn removes 6 vertices from an 8-vertex directed graph before its queue empties. How many vertices remain unprocessed?',2,'Two remain. The graph contains a cycle, though not every remaining vertex must lie on a cycle.')
mc(8,'For strictly increasing LIS, binary search finds:','The first tails entry ≥ the current value|The first tails entry > the current value|The last tails entry ≥ the current value|The last tails entry > the current value',0,'Lower_bound preserves strict increase; upper_bound would allow equal values in a nondecreasing subsequence.')
num(8,'What is the edit distance between “cat” and “cut” with unit insertion, deletion, substitution costs?',1,'Substitute a→u. Zero edits cannot change unequal strings, so 1 is optimal.')
mc(8,'Why iterate capacities downward in one-dimensional 0/1 knapsack?','To sort items by decreasing weight|To avoid using the current item more than once|To avoid considering empty capacity|To reconstruct the items without extra storage',1,'dp[w−weight] must still refer to the previous item layer, not an update made for the current item.')
num(8,'What is the strictly increasing LIS length of [3,5,7,1,2,8]?',4,'One LIS is [3,5,7,8]. The tails array can be [1,2,7,8] without itself being an input subsequence.')
mc(9,'A fixed-length rolling hash and precomputed leading power are known. Computing the next window hash takes:','O(n)|O(1)|O(m)|O(log m)',1,'Remove the leading contribution, multiply by the base, and add the incoming symbol with constant modular arithmetic.')
mc(9,'Why is polynomial-hash base p=1 problematic?','It makes modular multiplication linear|It prevents computing any prefix hash|It makes anagrams collide systematically|It requires a prime-length pattern',2,'All positional weights become 1, so order information disappears.')
num(9,'For pattern “ababaca”, what is π[4] using zero-based indexing?',3,'The prefix “ababa” has longest proper border “aba”, length 3.')
mc(9,'After a full KMP match, how do you preserve overlapping matches?','Set matched length to π[m−1]|Set matched length to m|Move text index back by m|Set matched length to zero',0,'The longest proper border may already match a prefix of the next occurrence.')
mc(10,'Aho–Corasick is primarily designed for:','One pattern with approximate matching|Many patterns in one text scan|One longest increasing subsequence|Sorting all suffixes of a text',1,'A trie plus failure and output links shares work across a dictionary of patterns.')
mc(10,'Prefix doubling sorts suffixes by:','Complete suffix strings in every round|Pairs of ranks of consecutive blocks|Only their first character each round|Pairs of randomly assigned priorities',1,'Two length-k block ranks represent a length-2k prefix, with a sentinel for missing second blocks.')
num(10,'Using LCP[r]=lcp(SA[r],SA[r−1]), LCP[0]=0, what is the maximum LCP for “banana”?',3,'The adjacent suffixes “ana” and “anana” share “ana”.')
mc(10,'Why is Kasai linear after the suffix array is available?','Every suffix comparison starts at zero|The carried common-prefix length falls by at most one per text step|The suffix array contains only equal-length suffixes|Binary search replaces all character comparisons',1,'The carried length rises only O(n) times in total and falls at most O(n) times.')
